EXTRA = {
'Construction and Technical Services': {
'offer': (
"Define the exact jobs you will sell before you quote anyone. List three or four services, such as small renovations, electrical repairs, waterproofing or technical inspections, and describe what is included and what is not. Compare what neighbours in Sao Paulo already offer and choose one gap you can fill well. Calculate the cost of each job in materials, labour, transport and waste, then add a margin for rework and delays. Draft a one-page scope sheet with photos, steps and a delivery date so clients understand the offer. Check the current official text on licences, technical responsibility and safety rules for your service type.",
"A bathroom waterproofing job needs R$ 380 in materials, R$ 450 in labour for two helpers and R$ 70 in transport. Direct cost is 380 + 450 + 70 = R$ 900. You add 15 percent for rework and delays: 900 x 0.15 = R$ 135, so total cost is R$ 1,035. If you want a 25 percent margin on the price, divide by 0.75: 1,035 / 0.75 = R$ 1,380. The written scope sheet lists the price as R$ 1,380."),
'people': (
"Plan who does what on each site. Identify the skills you need, such as masonry, electrical, plumbing and site supervision, and assess which ones you hold and which you must bring in. Design a simple checklist for safety equipment, tool care and daily cleaning. Explain the work order to every helper before starting, and demonstrate the standard you expect with one finished sample. Evaluate each helper after every job with three questions: on time, clean, correct. Plan client service too: confirm the visit time by message, report progress with photos, and apologise early when a delay happens.",
"You hire one helper at R$ 160 per day for a job that takes 6 days, so labour is 160 x 6 = R$ 960. Training time on day one cuts output by half a day, which costs 160 x 0.5 = R$ 80. A written checklist avoids one repeat visit worth R$ 120 in transport and lost time. Net benefit of the checklist and short training is 120 - 80 = R$ 40 on the first job and R$ 120 on each later job."),
'growth': (
"Measure each job, not only the month. Record quote value, real cost, days used and client satisfaction, then calculate margin per job and quote-to-contract rate. Compare your best and worst jobs and explain why they differ. Ask every client for a short written opinion and one referral, and keep a list of past clients to contact twice a year for maintenance. Evaluate which service brings the highest margin per day and give it more space in your offer. Plan renewal through maintenance visits and small follow-up jobs, and review your price list every quarter against material costs.",
"In one month you sent 20 quotes and won 8, so the win rate is 8 / 20 = 40 percent. Total revenue was R$ 14,400 and total direct costs were R$ 10,080. Margin is 14,400 - 10,080 = R$ 4,320, or 4,320 / 14,400 = 30 percent. If better follow-up lifts the win rate to 50 percent on the same 20 quotes, you win 10 jobs. At the same average of R$ 1,800 per job, revenue becomes R$ 18,000."),
},
'Winery': {
'offer': (
"Design the wine range around what you can produce consistently, not around every style you admire. Define two or three products, for example a young red, a white and a sparkling, and describe the buyer for each: restaurants, gift buyers or visitors to a tasting room. Calculate the cost per bottle including grapes, bottles, corks, labels, tanks, labour and losses. Compare your shelf price with similar wines in Sao Paulo shops. Plan tastings or small farm visits as part of the offer. Check the current official text on production registration, labelling and alcohol sales before you sell anything.",
"A batch of 1,000 bottles costs R$ 9,000 in grapes, R$ 4,500 in bottles, corks and labels, and R$ 3,500 in labour and energy. Total is 9,000 + 4,500 + 3,500 = R$ 17,000, so cost per bottle is R$ 17. Expecting 5 percent loss, you can sell 950 bottles, so real cost per sellable bottle is 17,000 / 950 = about R$ 17.89. A price of R$ 45 leaves roughly R$ 27.11 per bottle before taxes and selling costs."),
'people': (
"Identify the roles in a small winery: harvest helpers, cellar worker, bottling team, and a person who welcomes visitors and sells. List the skills each role needs, such as cleaning discipline, temperature control and clear tasting language. Train seasonal helpers with a one-page procedure and demonstrate each step once. Assess hygiene daily, because one careless cleaning can spoil a batch. Practise a short tasting talk that explains the wine in plain words without pressure. Plan for the busy harvest weeks by agreeing days and pay in writing early, and check the current official text on seasonal work.",
"Harvest needs 6 helpers for 4 days at R$ 150 per person per day: 6 x 4 x 150 = R$ 3,600. A trained helper picks 10 percent more than an untrained one. Without training you would need 4.4 helper-days extra to match, since 24 / 1.1 = about 21.8 days instead of 24, saving about 2.2 days. At R$ 150 a day, that is R$ 330 saved, which covers a R$ 100 training hour with R$ 230 to spare."),
'growth': (
"Measure sales by channel: direct visits, restaurants, online orders and events. Record bottles sold, average price, cost and unpaid invoices for each channel, and compare margins. Evaluate which wine sells out first and which stays on the shelf, then adjust the next batch. Build a club of repeat buyers by collecting contacts with consent and offering a seasonal release. Plan renewal with restaurant buyers through a tasting each quarter. Track stock per vintage so you can age or discount slow wines in time. Review prices once a year against grape and bottle costs.",
"Last quarter you sold 300 bottles directly at R$ 50 and 200 bottles to restaurants at R$ 36. Direct revenue is 300 x 50 = R$ 15,000 and restaurant revenue is 200 x 36 = R$ 7,200, total R$ 22,200. With a cost of R$ 18 per bottle, margin is 22,200 - (500 x 18) = 22,200 - 9,000 = R$ 13,200. Direct sales give R$ 32 per bottle against R$ 18 for restaurants, so you test two more tasting days."),
},
'Export and Import Business': {
'offer': (
"Define one product, one origin or destination market and one type of buyer before you start. Compare similar products already on offer and identify what you do better: price, quality, delivery time or small order size. Calculate the landed cost, adding product price, freight, insurance, taxes, customs agent, storage and exchange rate effect. Draft a clear offer sheet with minimum order, price terms, delivery time and payment terms. Test interest with a small sample order before a container. Check the current official text on licences, product rules and tax treatment, and ask a qualified customs professional to confirm your costs.",
"You plan to import 500 units at R$ 40 each: R$ 20,000. Freight and insurance add R$ 3,000, import taxes add R$ 5,500, and the customs agent and storage add R$ 1,500. Landed cost is 20,000 + 3,000 + 5,500 + 1,500 = R$ 30,000, or R$ 60 per unit. To keep a 30 percent margin on price, divide by 0.70: 60 / 0.70 = about R$ 85.71. You set the price at R$ 86."),
'people': (
"Identify who handles each step: supplier contact, documents, transport, customs, payment and client service. Assess which tasks you can learn and which need a specialist, such as a customs broker or freight forwarder. Create a checklist of documents for every shipment and review it before the goods leave. Practise clear written communication with suppliers, using short messages, confirmed dates and photos. Explain delays to your clients early and offer options. Evaluate every partner on punctuality, accuracy and clear answers, and keep a backup for each key role. Record every agreement in writing and confirm it by message.",
"A shipment depends on 4 partners. If each is on time 95 percent of the time, the chance all four are on time is 0.95 x 0.95 x 0.95 x 0.95 = about 0.815, or 81.5 percent. Adding a document checklist that raises each partner to 98 percent gives 0.98 to the power of four, about 0.922. A late shipment costs R$ 1,200 in storage and penalties, so expected delay cost drops from 0.185 x 1,200 = R$ 222 to 0.078 x 1,200 = R$ 94."),
'growth': (
"Measure each shipment: product cost, landed cost, selling price, days from order to cash and margin. Compare results by supplier and by client, and evaluate which relationship gives the most stable profit. Track exchange rate movement because it changes your cost between order and payment. Plan renewal by agreeing repeat orders with clients and annual terms with suppliers. Review your stock days, since goods sitting on a shelf hold cash. Grow by adding one related product only after the first line pays back its costs. Check the current official text before entering a new market.",
"A shipment cost R$ 30,000 landed and sold for R$ 42,000 over 60 days. Margin is 42,000 - 30,000 = R$ 12,000, or 12,000 / 42,000 = about 28.6 percent. If the exchange rate rises 5 percent between order and payment on the R$ 20,000 product part, you pay R$ 1,000 more, so margin falls to R$ 11,000 or about 26.2 percent. You decide to quote prices valid for 30 days only."),
},
'Financing Start-ups': {
'offer': (
"Design your financing offer around a clear problem. Define who you help, for example founders who need a simple cash plan, a loan comparison or a pitch to a partner. List the services: cash flow templates, workshops, review of documents and introductions to public support programmes. Compare the options a founder has: own savings, family, partners, bank credit, public programmes and grants, and explain the cost and risk of each in plain words. Calculate what your service costs you to deliver. Never promise returns or approval. Check the current official text of any programme before you describe its rules.",
"A founder needs R$ 30,000 and compares two loans over 12 months. Loan A has total repayments of R$ 34,800 and loan B has R$ 33,600. The total cost of A is 34,800 - 30,000 = R$ 4,800 and of B is 33,600 - 30,000 = R$ 3,600. Loan B costs R$ 1,200 less, or R$ 100 less per month over 12 months. Monthly payment of B is 33,600 / 12 = R$ 2,800."),
'people': (
"Identify the people who help a start-up raise money: the founder, an accountant, a mentor, a partner and a lender contact. Assess which skills the founder lacks, such as reading a cash flow or explaining numbers calmly, and plan short practice sessions. Practise a three-minute pitch with a clear problem, customer, price and use of funds. Create a list of questions a lender will ask and rehearse honest answers. Explain risks openly, because trust matters more than polish. Evaluate each practice round with a simple score on clarity, numbers and confidence.",
"A founder rehearses a pitch 5 times. Scores out of 10 for clarity are 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8. The average of all rounds is (4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 8) / 5 = 6. The gain from the first to the last round is 8 - 4 = 4 points, or 100 percent. If each practice session takes 1 hour and you value your time at R$ 80 per hour, the five sessions cost 5 x 80 = R$ 400."),
'growth': (
"Measure the health of the start-up after funding, not only at the start. Track monthly cash in, cash out, runway in months and repayment dates. Calculate runway by dividing cash by the average monthly net spending. Compare actual results with the plan each month and explain differences. Evaluate every new expense against the question: does it bring customers or save money soon? Plan renewal conversations with lenders and partners early, bringing updated numbers. Keep records tidy so any review is easy. Never rely on future growth to cover today's payments.",
"A start-up has R$ 24,000 in the bank. Monthly sales are R$ 9,000 and monthly spending is R$ 14,000, so net cash use is 14,000 - 9,000 = R$ 5,000. Runway is 24,000 / 5,000 = 4.8 months. If you cut spending by R$ 1,500, net use becomes R$ 3,500 and runway becomes 24,000 / 3,500 = about 6.9 months, giving two extra months to find customers."),
},
'Food Trucking': {
'offer': (
"Design a short menu of five to seven items that you can prepare fast in a small kitchen. Define your signature dish and the customer you serve: office workers at lunch, students or event crowds. Compare menus and prices of nearby trucks and food stalls. Calculate the cost of each item, including ingredients, packaging, gas and waste, then set prices to cover costs and profit. Test the menu for two weeks and remove slow items. Plan the places and hours with the best foot traffic. Check the current official text on street food permits, vehicle rules and food handling before you open.",
"A burger costs R$ 7.50 in ingredients and R$ 1.50 in packaging and gas share, so direct cost is R$ 9.00. You want the cost to be 35 percent of the price, so price = 9.00 / 0.35 = about R$ 25.71. You round to R$ 26. If you sell 60 burgers on a day, revenue is 60 x 26 = R$ 1,560 and direct cost is 60 x 9 = R$ 540, leaving R$ 1,020 before staff, fuel and permits."),
'people': (
"Plan a small, reliable team for the truck: one cook, one cashier and one person for cleaning and stock. Identify tasks for each role and write a short opening and closing routine. Train every person in food handling, hand washing and safe storage, and assess them with a practical demonstration. Practise fast, friendly service with a line of customers, using simple phrases and clear order repeating. Create a plan for rainy days and sick staff. Evaluate service time per customer and aim to reduce it without losing quality. Check the current official text on hygiene training and employment rules.",
"Your truck serves 40 customers in a lunch peak of 80 minutes, which is 2 minutes per customer. After redesigning the station, service time drops to 1.5 minutes. In 80 minutes you can now serve 80 / 1.5 = about 53 customers, 13 more. At an average ticket of R$ 28, the extra sales are 13 x 28 = R$ 364 per lunch peak, or R$ 1,820 over five days."),
'growth': (
"Measure each day: customers served, average ticket, items sold, waste and location. Compare locations and hours, then evaluate which ones bring the best profit per hour, not just the biggest crowd. Collect contacts with consent to announce where the truck will be. Plan renewal through loyalty cards, weekly specials and partnerships with offices or event organisers. Review ingredient prices monthly and adjust menu or portions. Keep maintenance money aside for the vehicle. Grow by adding a second shift or location only after your records show steady margins for at least two months.",
"At location A you sell R$ 1,500 in 5 hours with R$ 600 of costs. Profit is 1,500 - 600 = R$ 900, or R$ 180 per hour. At location B you sell R$ 1,200 in 3 hours with R$ 460 of costs. Profit is 1,200 - 460 = R$ 740, or about R$ 247 per hour. Location B is better per hour, so you use the freed 2 hours for a new spot test or for preparation."),
},
'Bed and Breakfast Business': {
'offer': (
"Define what makes your stay different: a quiet garden, a homemade breakfast, a location near a neighbourhood, or a warm host. Describe your guest, such as a visiting worker, a couple or a traveller on a short trip. List what is included: room, breakfast, bathroom, internet and local tips. Compare nightly prices of similar stays in Sao Paulo. Calculate your cost per night, including cleaning, laundry, breakfast, energy and platform costs. Draft clear house rules and cancellation terms. Check the current official text on registration of lodging, taxes and guest registration before you accept bookings.",
"One room has these costs per guest-night: breakfast R$ 12, cleaning and laundry R$ 35, energy and water R$ 8, so variable cost is 12 + 35 + 8 = R$ 55. You set the price at R$ 200. Contribution per night is 200 - 55 = R$ 145. If monthly fixed costs for the room are R$ 1,450, you need 1,450 / 145 = 10 occupied nights each month to cover them."),
'people': (
"Identify the tasks that create a good stay: welcoming, cleaning, breakfast, repairs and answering messages. Assess which tasks you do and which you can share with a cleaner or a helper. Create a room checklist with photos so the standard stays the same every day. Practise a clear check-in routine: greet the guest, explain the house, show the wifi and hand over the keys. Respond to messages within a set time. Evaluate guest comments weekly and fix one thing each week. Treat every guest with respect and protect their privacy and personal data.",
"A cleaner takes 90 minutes to turn a room without a checklist and 60 minutes with one. At R$ 20 per hour, cost falls from 1.5 x 20 = R$ 30 to 1 x 20 = R$ 20, saving R$ 10 per turnover. With 15 turnovers a month, savings are 15 x 10 = R$ 150 monthly. Over a year that is 150 x 12 = R$ 1,800, plus fewer complaints about missed items."),
'growth': (
"Measure occupancy, average nightly price, revenue per available night and guest score. Calculate occupancy by dividing nights sold by nights available. Compare weekdays with weekends and seasons, then evaluate when to raise or lower prices. Ask each guest for honest feedback and a review, and answer reviews politely. Plan renewal with returning guests through direct contact and a small welcome-back gesture. Keep a maintenance fund for paint, linen and repairs. Grow slowly: improve one room fully before adding another, and check the numbers every month. Write down what changed and why each time.",
"In a 30-night month your room was occupied 18 nights at R$ 200. Occupancy is 18 / 30 = 60 percent and revenue is 18 x 200 = R$ 3,600. Revenue per available night is 3,600 / 30 = R$ 120. If a weekday offer at R$ 170 fills 4 more nights, revenue grows by 4 x 170 = R$ 680 to R$ 4,280, and occupancy becomes 22 / 30 = about 73 percent."),
},
'Hostel or Hotel Business': {
'offer': (
"Design your stay around one clear guest type, such as budget travellers, business visitors or groups. Define room types, shared spaces and services like breakfast, luggage storage and laundry. Compare prices, ratings and location of nearby places and identify your advantage. Calculate cost per occupied bed or room, including staff, cleaning, utilities, maintenance and booking platform costs. Draft clear rules on check-in, noise, cancellation and safety. Plan the bed mix: dormitory beds fill faster, private rooms earn more per night. Check the current official text on lodging registration, fire safety, accessibility and guest records.",
"A hostel has 20 beds. Average price is R$ 70 per bed per night, and monthly costs are R$ 30,000. Break-even occupied bed-nights are 30,000 / 70 = about 429. Available bed-nights in 30 days are 20 x 30 = 600. Break-even occupancy is 429 / 600 = about 71.5 percent. If you expect 65 percent occupancy, revenue is 0.65 x 600 x 70 = R$ 27,300, a gap of R$ 2,700 to close."),
'people': (
"Plan shifts so the front desk, cleaning and maintenance are covered at the right hours. Identify the skills each role needs: welcoming, languages, problem solving, cleaning standards and basic safety. Create written routines for check-in, room inspection and emergencies, and practise them with the team. Assess each person with real situations, such as an overbooked night or a noisy guest. Explain how to treat every guest equally and with respect. Evaluate staff workload, because tired teams make mistakes. Check the current official text on working hours, rest and contracts for every role.",
"A front desk needs 24-hour cover, which is 168 hours per week. One person works 44 hours, so you need 168 / 44 = about 3.8 people, rounded up to 4. At R$ 2,200 per month each, the base wages are 4 x 2,200 = R$ 8,800 before any extra costs. If a night self check-in system lets you cover 10 night hours with remote support, you reduce the need by 70 hours a month."),
'growth': (
"Measure occupancy, average daily rate, revenue per available bed or room, cancellations and review scores. Compare channels: direct bookings, platforms and groups, and calculate the commission cost of each. Evaluate seasons and events in Sao Paulo to set prices by demand. Plan renewal with corporate agreements, repeat guests and local partners such as tour guides. Track maintenance requests and fix repeated problems at the root. Review costs monthly and keep a reserve for repairs. Add rooms or services only when occupancy stays above your break-even point for several months. Keep notes on every price change you test.",
"A platform charges 15 percent commission on a R$ 280 room-night, so you keep 280 x 0.85 = R$ 238. A direct booking at R$ 260 has a 3 percent payment cost, leaving 260 x 0.97 = R$ 252.20. The direct booking earns 252.20 - 238 = R$ 14.20 more, even at a lower price. If 40 nights per month move from platform to direct, you gain 40 x 14.20 = R$ 568."),
},
'Carpentry': {
'offer': (
"Define the products or services you will sell: made-to-measure furniture, repairs, kitchen cabinets, doors or small decorative items. Choose a niche where your skill is strongest and compare what others in your area charge. Calculate the cost of each piece, including wood, hardware, finish, electricity, tool wear, labour hours and transport. Draft a simple quote with drawing, materials, delivery date and payment terms. Plan a small catalogue of standard items that you can repeat efficiently. Test one new product with a few customers first. Check the current official text on wood origin, safety and business registration.",
"A bookshelf uses R$ 260 in wood, R$ 40 in hardware and finish, and 12 hours of work valued at R$ 25 per hour, which is R$ 300. Total cost is 260 + 40 + 300 = R$ 600. Adding R$ 30 for transport and waste gives R$ 630. For a 30 percent margin on price, divide by 0.70: 630 / 0.70 = R$ 900. The quote to the customer is R$ 900."),
'people': (
"Identify the skills in your shop: measuring, cutting, joining, finishing and installing. Assess which ones limit your speed and plan practice or a helper for them. Create a safety routine for machines, dust, noise and protective equipment, and review it before every working day. Demonstrate each step when you teach an assistant, then watch them repeat it. Practise listening to the client, repeating measures and confirming choices in writing. Evaluate quality with a final checklist for dimensions, finish and fitting. Plan clear communication on delivery dates, and warn early if wood or hardware is delayed.",
"A helper at R$ 90 per day shortens a 12-hour piece to 8 hours of your time because he cuts and sands. Your time saved is 4 hours at R$ 25, worth R$ 100. If he works half a day on the piece, his cost is R$ 45. Net gain is 100 - 45 = R$ 55 per piece. Over 6 pieces a month, the helper adds 6 x 55 = R$ 330 in value."),
'growth': (
"Measure the time and money of every job: hours used, material cost, quote value and final margin. Compare quoted hours with real hours and evaluate where you lose time. Collect photos of finished work and ask clients for a short opinion. Plan renewal through maintenance visits, small repairs and referrals. Build relationships with designers, builders and shops that need a reliable carpenter. Review wood and hardware prices every month and update your price list. Invest in a new machine only when your records show that it saves hours you can sell.",
"Last month you completed 5 jobs with quoted hours totalling 60 but real hours totalling 75. You lost 15 hours, worth 15 x 25 = R$ 375. Next month you add 20 percent to estimated hours for jobs of the same type: 60 x 1.2 = 72 hours, close to the real figure. Quotes rise by about 12 hours x 25 = R$ 300 across the five jobs, which protects your margin."),
},
'Event Organization and Management': {
'offer': (
"Define the type of event you organise: birthdays, weddings, company meetings or community festivals. List what is included in each package: venue search, suppliers, schedule, decoration and day-of coordination. Compare packages from other organisers in Sao Paulo and identify your difference. Calculate the cost of each package, including supplier fees, your hours, transport and a contingency reserve. Draft a clear contract summary with deadlines, payment dates and what happens if the date changes. Check the current official text on venue permits, music rights, safety and contracts before you sign anything.",
"A 60-guest birthday package has supplier costs of R$ 9,000 for food, R$ 2,500 for the venue and R$ 1,500 for decoration, total R$ 13,000. You add a 10 percent contingency of R$ 1,300 and 25 hours of your time at R$ 40 per hour, which is R$ 1,000. Total cost is 13,000 + 1,300 + 1,000 = R$ 15,300. With a 20 percent margin on price: 15,300 / 0.80 = R$ 19,125, or about R$ 319 per guest."),
'people': (
"Identify the roles for each event: coordinator, suppliers, helpers, security and guest reception. Create a run sheet that lists every task with a time and a responsible person. Explain it to everyone in a short briefing before the event, and practise the opening and the first hour. Assess supplier reliability by asking for references and visiting a previous job when possible. Plan a backup for the most critical suppliers, like sound and food. Evaluate how the team handles problems and hold a short review afterward. Communicate with the client in writing after every decision.",
"You need 6 helpers for 8 hours at R$ 20 per hour: 6 x 8 x 20 = R$ 960. A good run sheet lets you manage with 5 helpers because tasks are better planned. Cost becomes 5 x 8 x 20 = R$ 800, saving R$ 160 per event. With 10 events a year, savings are R$ 1,600, and the extra planning time of 2 hours per event costs 10 x 2 x 40 = R$ 800, so the net gain is R$ 800."),
'growth': (
"Measure each event: budget against real cost, guest numbers, supplier delays, client score and margin. Compare events by type and evaluate which one gives the best profit per hour of your work. Ask clients for feedback and photos, with permission to use them. Plan renewal through anniversary reminders, yearly company events and referrals from suppliers. Keep a list of trusted suppliers with prices and ratings. Review your pricing twice a year. Grow by recruiting a regular helper or specialising in the event type that repeats most often, and check cash flow before each large deposit to suppliers.",
"Two event types last year: weddings gave R$ 6,000 profit for 80 hours of work, which is R$ 75 per hour. Company meetings gave R$ 3,200 profit for 25 hours, which is R$ 128 per hour. Company meetings pay more per hour. If you move 100 hours from weddings to meetings, you earn about 100 x 128 = R$ 12,800 instead of 100 x 75 = R$ 7,500, a gain of R$ 5,300, if demand exists."),
},
'Franchise Business': {
'offer': (
"Define what you are actually buying or selling in a franchise: a brand, a method, training, supply and support. Compare at least three franchise options and list what each requires: initial cost, monthly royalties, marketing contributions, space and staff. Calculate your expected monthly sales, costs and break-even, using conservative numbers and not the highest example shown. Ask for the circular of offer, speak with current owners and check the current official text on franchise disclosure. Evaluate whether the model fits your skills and local market. Never rely on promises of profit; seek a qualified adviser to review the contract.",
"A franchise asks R$ 90,000 to start, plus 6 percent royalties and 2 percent marketing on sales. Expected monthly sales are R$ 60,000. Royalties and marketing are 60,000 x 0.08 = R$ 4,800. Other monthly costs are R$ 42,000 for goods, staff, rent and utilities. Monthly result is 60,000 - 4,800 - 42,000 = R$ 13,200 before taxes and owner pay. If sales are 20 percent lower at R$ 48,000, the result changes sharply, so test that case too."),
'people': (
"Identify the skills needed to run the unit: sales, team leadership, cash control and following a standard method. Assess your own strengths and decide who you must hire. Plan the training offered by the franchisor and list what you must practise yourself. Create daily routines that follow the manual: opening, service, stock and closing. Explain the standards to your team using examples and short checklists. Evaluate staff by the same measures the network uses. Communicate with the franchisor clearly, and keep written records of requests and answers. Check the current official text on employment obligations.",
"A unit needs 5 employees at R$ 2,000 per month each. With social charges of about 70 percent, the cost per employee is 2,000 x 1.7 = R$ 3,400 and for five is R$ 17,000 a month. If better scheduling means one fewer part-time shift worth R$ 1,200 per month, the saving is R$ 1,200 x 12 = R$ 14,400 a year. Check the current official text for the real charge rates."),
'growth': (
"Measure the unit against the network targets: sales, average ticket, customer count, costs and customer satisfaction. Compare your results with the monthly break-even and evaluate gaps. Plan local marketing within the franchise rules, such as neighbourhood partnerships and loyalty offers. Keep contact lists of regular clients with consent. Review contract dates for renewal, transfer and territory, and prepare numbers in advance. Evaluate whether to open a second unit only after steady results and a clear understanding of your debts and cash needs. Keep a cash reserve for slow months, and write down what you will do if sales fall.",
"Your unit has monthly break-even sales of R$ 52,000. Actual sales over four months were R$ 49,000, R$ 53,000, R$ 56,000 and R$ 58,000. The average is (49,000 + 53,000 + 56,000 + 58,000) / 4 = R$ 54,000. That is 54,000 - 52,000 = R$ 2,000 above break-even, or about 3.8 percent. A drop of only 4 percent would bring you back below break-even, so you keep a reserve."),
},
'Manufacturing Business': {
'offer': (
"Define the product you will make, the customer who needs it and the quality standard you can repeat. Compare similar products on price, materials and delivery time, and identify where you can be better. Calculate unit cost from materials, labour, energy, machine time, packaging and scrap. Draft a product sheet with specifications, minimum order and lead time. Plan a pilot batch before full production and test it with real buyers. Decide whether to make everything or outsource steps. Check the current official text on product standards, labelling, environmental rules and tax treatment for your product type.",
"A batch of 200 bags costs R$ 3,200 in materials, R$ 1,800 in labour, R$ 400 in energy and R$ 200 in packaging, total R$ 5,600. Scrap of 5 percent leaves 190 good bags, so cost per good bag is 5,600 / 190 = about R$ 29.47. For a 35 percent margin on price, divide by 0.65: 29.47 / 0.65 = about R$ 45.34. You set the wholesale price at R$ 46."),
'people': (
"Identify roles on the production line: operators, quality checker, stock handler and supervisor. Create written work instructions with photos for each step, and demonstrate them on the machine. Assess operators by observing a full cycle and checking the output against the standard. Plan safety training for machines, noise, dust and lifting, and confirm that protective equipment is used. Practise handover between shifts with a short log. Evaluate defects by cause, not by blame, and ask workers for improvement ideas. Check the current official text on workplace safety and employment rules.",
"A line makes 400 units per shift with 8 percent defects, so good units are 400 x 0.92 = 368. After clearer work instructions, defects fall to 5 percent: 400 x 0.95 = 380 good units. That is 12 more good units per shift. At a cost of R$ 18 per unit, you avoid 12 x 18 = R$ 216 in waste per shift, or R$ 4,320 over 20 working days."),
'growth': (
"Measure output per hour, defect rate, material waste, on-time delivery and cost per unit. Compare each month and evaluate the largest cause of loss. Plan renewal with customers through regular orders, quality reports and quick answers to complaints. Track machine maintenance so stops are planned, not sudden. Review supplier prices and look for a second source for critical materials. Calculate capacity before accepting a large order. Grow by improving yield and delivery first, then add a product or shift when machines and cash allow it. Document each change so the team can repeat it.",
"Your machine runs 160 hours a month and makes 20 units per hour, so maximum output is 3,200 units. Last month you produced 2,400, which is 2,400 / 3,200 = 75 percent of capacity. A new customer wants 500 units monthly. Total would be 2,900, or about 90.6 percent of capacity. At a contribution of R$ 12 per unit, the order adds 500 x 12 = R$ 6,000 per month, leaving little room for breakdowns."),
},
}
